    <title>Fixed, Proportional, or Menu-Based? A Study of Managed Security Service Provider Contracts</title>
    <updated>2026-10-12T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Yong Wu, Yujie Jiang, Xinxue (Shawn) Qu, Qianzhou Du, Zhengrui Jiang</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Information Systems Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Cybersecurity threats are increasing the demand for managed security services, yet service providers face a fundamental challenge: how to design contracts that encourage client participation while reducing risks arising from security externalities. This study shows that offering a menu of security contracts can serve as a strategic tool for both market expansion and risk management. By providing both fixed and proportional compensation options simultaneously, MSSPs can leverage client self-selection to better manage heterogeneous risks. As security risks increase, high-valuation clients may migrate toward proportional compensation contracts, reducing aggregate client negligence and lowering MSSPs’ compensation burdens, which enables market expansion and higher profitability. Under certain market conditions, this flexible contract design creates a “win-win-win” outcome, benefiting providers, clients, and society. Moreover, our findings reveal that market incentives do not always align with social objectives. We identify the conditions under which targeted policy interventions—such as contractual guidelines, subsidies, or restrictions—may be needed to realign private incentives with social welfare. These insights highlight the importance of aligning cybersecurity service design and regulation with both economic incentives and collective security outcomes.</content>
