    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/isre.2025.2343</id>
    <title>Honesty in Causal Forests: When It Helps and When It Hurts</title>
    <updated>2026-10-14T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Yanfang Hou, Carlos Fernández-Loría</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Information Systems Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Causal forests are increasingly used to estimate who benefits most from an intervention, informing targeting and personalization in business and public policy. Widely used software often applies a safeguard called “honest estimation” by default. It splits the data: one part is used to find groups of people who respond differently to the intervention, and another to estimate the effect within each group. Our key finding is that splitting the data reduces the risk of mistaking chance patterns for real ones, but it also makes real differences harder to detect, which can make estimates less accurate. Across more than 7,000 benchmark data sets, we found that honest estimation can require up to 27% more data to reach the same accuracy as causal forests that did not use it. The takeaway is that honesty should be a choice, not a default. Whether it helps depends on the data and what the estimates are needed for. For organizations and policymakers planning experiments, its potential data cost should also be part of the design conversation.</content>
