    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261498550</id>
    <title>Multi-self presentation and knowledge contribution in platform operations: Evidence from a natural experiment</title>
    <updated>2026-10-15T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Yingpeng Zhu, Zike Cao, Gen Li, Yi Yang, Liangfei Qiu</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Production and Operations Management&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Operations of online user-generated content (UGC) platforms often involve tacklingunder-provision, wherein users lack motivation to contribute sufficient content. We study a novel design deployed to address this issue: the integration of a proprietary social feature that allows users to post casual free-form content (e.g., casual social content) alongside standard formal content on the platform. Leveraging a quasi-experiment on a large Chinese online knowledge-sharing platform, we assess the effects of integrating such a social feature on users’ formal knowledge contribution (i.e., writinganswersto others’questions). Using difference-in-differences (DiD) analyses paired with various matching strategies, such as propensity score matching and look-ahead propensity score matching, we find that users who adopt and frequently use the feature (i.e., high adopters) show a stronger incentive to contributeanswerswithout compromising quality. These results are further validated by a set of robustness checks. Further mediation analysis suggests that such a complementary effect might be because of two mechanisms related to the integrated social feature’smulti-self presentationfunction: first, the new feature increases high adopters’ engagement on the platform and leads them to consume more knowledge content, which in turn facilitates knowledge contribution; second, high adopters receive more social recognition benefits by building a more complete self through the integrated social feature, and such increased social recognition benefits further motivate more subsequent content contribution. Moreover, employing a text-mining approach to assess the semantic similarity between users’ casual social content and their formal knowledge contributions, we find that the complementary effect is amplified for high adopters whose casual posts remain more closely connected to their formal knowledge domain (i.e., are more semantically aligned with their formal knowledge). Our results have novel implications for platform design and operations to motivate users to contribute valuable content.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261498550"/>
    <category term="Production and Operations Management"/>
    <published>2026-10-15T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261498518</id>
    <title>EXPRESS: Pricing and Information Provision in Service Platforms with Heterogeneous Customers</title>
    <updated>2026-10-15T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Xin Weng, Lijian Lu, Li Xiao</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Production and Operations Management&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Service platforms frequently employ virtual queues where customers have differing sensitivities to wait times and cannot see their precise positions in line. In these settings, providers often disclose waiting-time information to influence customers' decisions on whether to join or balk. A natural benchmark is to report the long-term average waiting time, yet whether this deterministic disclosure policy is optimal remains unclear when both pricing and customer participation are endogenous. This study develops a stylized theoretical framework to explore the joint structure of pricing and waiting-time information disclosure for service providers managing diverse customers in a virtual queue. The provider decides on both the service price and the waiting-time disclosure policy within a rational-expectations framework. Our findings reveal that the optimal disclosure strategy is captured by scaled Rademacher distributions, meaning at most two distinct announcement values are required. This reduces a complex infinite-dimensional problem to a more manageable finite-dimensional optimization task. Leveraging this framework, we derive the optimal strategies for pricing and disclosure, identifying scenarios where variability in announced waiting times is beneficial. Our analysis shows that noise is advantageous when customers can handle uncertain wait-time signals or initially perceive the service as quick. The optimal level of noise correlates with system congestion, offering the greatest benefits when there is a notable disparity between perceived and actual congestion levels. This provides significant advantages when disclosing waiting times with noise. These findings offer actionable insights for service platforms, guiding them in creating cohesive strategies that encompass pricing, information disclosure, and service configuration, ultimately enhancing customer experience and operational efficiency.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261498518"/>
    <category term="Production and Operations Management"/>
    <published>2026-10-15T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261499473</id>
    <title>EXPRESS: Valuation Bottlenecks: Why Isn’t a Better Business Enough for a Higher Market Capitalization?</title>
    <updated>2026-10-15T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Shumpei Iwao, Vatsal Mitesh Tailor, Qi Wu</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Production and Operations Management&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Firms often improve operations, disclosure, governance, or financing capacity without an immediate and proportional market-capitalization response. Could a single unresolved concern limit the market's response to improvements elsewhere in the firm? Classical multifactor and machine-learning models ask which risks or characteristics explain or predict returns. We ask whether the relatively weakest among several coexisting firm conditions receives disproportionate valuation weight. We model equity value as the lower envelope of lens-specific valuations. A hard minimum makes one lens locally binding; a soft minimum assigns continuous, state-dependent weights. We use exploratory factor analysis on 268 accounting characteristics to construct five firm-state lenses, with membership and weights determined by the loading structure rather than by an economic taxonomy, and rank each lens within industry, size, and quarter peers. We document aweakest lens effect: beyond the additive effects of all five lens ranks, a firm's weakest lens carries an incremental association with next-quarter returns, and it survives saturated joint rank-quintile controls. Gating the test at the bottom decile and then the bottom quintile of the peer distribution, the association is concentrated among firms whose weakest lens is weak against peers and not merely lowest within the firm. In baseline soft-min specifications, it fades steadily as the gate is widened. We then investigate whether analyst attention may help explain this pattern. We use large language models on 1.7 million earnings-call questions and find that, in baseline specifications, the weakest lens attracts a larger share of questions, especially when it is also weak among peers; this cross-sectional alignment is consistent with an attention channel as one possible explanation for the observed return pattern. The framework offers managers seeking to maximize market capitalization a new perspective on prioritizing scarce managerial attention.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261499473"/>
    <category term="Production and Operations Management"/>
    <published>2026-10-15T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261498521</id>
    <title>Response efficiency versus response customization: An examination of firms’ responses to platform-empowered group complaints</title>
    <updated>2026-10-15T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Zilong Liu, Xuequn Wang, Xin (Robert) Luo, Chaoqun Chu, Tingting Song</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Production and Operations Management&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;The rise of group complaints on digital platforms, where consumers can publicly voice collective concerns, presents significant challenges for firms’ service recovery operations. Prior research assessing service operations on digital platforms has focused primarily on individual complaints, and less is known about how firms respond to platform-empowered (PE) group complaints. We examine this question through the lens of a trade-off between response efficiency and response customization and argue that this trade-off evolves as group complaints develop over time. Based on a large-scale dataset from Tousu, a leading complaint platform in China, we find that group complaints paradoxically increase response time and decrease empathy and explanation in firms’ responses. Further analyses indicate that these effects depend on dynamic group size and dynamic need diversity. When dynamic need diversity is low, larger groups are associated with more standardized, efficiency-oriented responses, whereas when dynamic need diversity is high, larger groups are associated with slower but more customized responses. These findings extend research on service operations by showing how PE group complaints reshape firms’ complaint handling. Our study also provides operational guidance for managing group complaints more effectively.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261498521"/>
    <category term="Production and Operations Management"/>
    <published>2026-10-15T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2025.2135</id>
    <title>Online Decisions with (Biased) Offline Data</title>
    <updated>2026-10-15T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Hyun-Soo Ahn, Mengzhenyu Zhang, Zijin Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Operations Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Making Better Decisions with Imperfect Historical DataHistorical data can provide valuable information for online decision making, but its usefulness may be limited when the environment has changed. In “Online Decisions with (Biased) Offline Data,” Ahn, Zhang, and Zhang study how firms can combine potentially biased offline data with newly collected online observations in two canonical operations management problems: dynamic inventory decisions and dynamic pricing. The authors develop two online learning policies that balance the benefits of historical information against the risk of relying on data from a different distribution. The soft-switch policy automatically adjusts the reliance on offline and online data without requiring prior knowledge of the degree of bias. The hard-switch policy instead uses information about the magnitude of the distributional shift to determine when to rely primarily on online data. Both policies achieve optimal regret up to a logarithmic factor, whereas the analysis quantifies how the value of offline data depends on its size and bias.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2025.2135"/>
    <category term="Operations Research"/>
    <published>2026-10-15T00:00:00+00:00</published>
