    <id>https://doi.org/10.1002/jcpy.70041</id>
    <title>Signaling uniqueness: The psycholinguistic effect of language precision on product perceptions</title>
    <updated>2026-10-04T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Guang‐Xin Xie, Ann Kronrod</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Journal of Consumer Psychology&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;This research reveals a psycholinguistic effect whereby more precise words in product communications increase consumer perceptions of product uniqueness. Drawing on Categorization Theory, the authors propose that more precise words in product descriptions are situated deeper within their semantic categories and denote narrower subsets of referents, thereby eliciting perceptions of product uniqueness. [Correction added on 15 October 2026, after first online publication: The reference in the preceding sentence has been removed.] Four experiments provide robust evidence supporting the “precise‐unique effect” and its underlying psycholinguistic mechanism driven by categorical depth. The findings further suggest that consumers' need for product uniqueness moderates the downstream effects of language precision on perceived attractiveness and behavioral intention via perceived uniqueness. This research contributes to the growing literature on language effects in consumer psychology and provides practical implications for making strategic lexical choices that enhance product positioning and differentiation.</content>
