  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1111/jofi.70083</id>
    <title>Flow‐Driven ESG Returns</title>
    <updated>2026-10-07T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>PHILIPPE VAN DER BECK</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;The Journal of Finance&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;I show that the recent returns to environmental, social, and governance (ESG) investing are strongly driven by price impact from flows toward ESG portfolios. Using data on trades, I estimate the market's ability to accommodate ESG flows, which is given by the elasticity of substitution between ESG and other stocks. I show that every dollar flowing toward a representative ESG portfolio increases the market value of ESG stocks by $0.8. The growing institutional flows into the ESG portfolio are the main driver of ESG returns and have caused an annual flow‐driven return of 1.9%. In the absence of flows, ESG stocks would not have outperformed the market from 2012 to 2023.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1111/jofi.70083"/>
    <category term="The Journal of Finance"/>
    <published>2026-10-07T00:00:00+00:00</published>
