    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/isre.2024.1215</id>
    <title>Membership Plans for Sharing Economy Platforms</title>
    <updated>2026-09-30T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Haozhao Zhang, Huseyin Cavusoglu, Srinivasan Raghunathan</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Information Systems Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Sharing economy platforms such as ride-hailing platforms increasingly experiment with membership plans for consumers, providers, or both. This research shows when and why these plans create value. We find that a consumer-side membership plan is profitable mainly when expected provider supply is large. A provider-side membership plan is profitable mainly when expected consumer demand is large. A both-sides membership plan is attractive only when neither demand nor supply potential is extreme. In addition, one-sided membership plans harm participants on the targeted side while benefiting the opposite side. Both-sides membership plans typically help one side at the expense of the other. Social welfare rises consistently under a consumer-side membership plan because more valuable matches occur, but provider-side or dual plans can generate inefficient matches and lower welfare when the provider base is large. These findings carry direct policy implications: regulatory mandates to reclassify regular providers as employees and guarantee fixed wages may reduce overall provider surplus and social welfare through inefficient matches. Therefore, platforms should treat membership design as a strategic response to demand- and supply-side conditions rather than a simple loyalty tool.</content>
